<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Onogurs</h1><p class="st-meta">463 CE – 632 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Oghur Turkic nomads of the Pontic steppe whose name survives in the word 'Hungary', and from whom the Bulgars of Kubrat emerged.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Onogurs, meaning 'ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghurs">Oghurs</a>', are first recorded in 463 when, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saragurs">Saragurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urogs">Urogs</a>, they sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> after being driven west by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sabirs">Sabirs</a>. They settled in the steppe between the Kuban, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Don</a>, in contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were a tribal confederation of pastoral nomads organised into ten tribes under chieftains. Byzantine writers record their dealings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and their conversion attempts; their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organa">Organa</a> and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a> had ties to the Byzantine court. In the late 6th century they came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/utigurs">Utigurs</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 632 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a> threw off Turkic rule and united Onogurs, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/utigurs">Utigurs</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>. After his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> scattered it, and his sons led groups to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. The Onogur name, through Slavic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> its name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Hungarians</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 463 CE – 632 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:68.92%;width:3.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>463 CE – 632 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Organa, Kubrat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oghur Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia, Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Onoghurs, Onogur confederation</dd></dl></aside></div>
